A||||8||Mach10||Auto trans "quirks"||||5||
Z||000000||Mach10||09-23-2001||07:24 PM||||Not sure if this is a major problem or not. doesn't seem to be...<P>If at any time during a drive, you floor the gas, and back off in a certain way BEFORE it shifts into second (I haven't quite determined the factors) the throttle will become "heavy" and the shifting characteristics of the tranny change dramatically. The throttle is harder to push (  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> ) and the shifts are delayed, and very hard. It will stay like this for the remainder of the drive. If you stop, and put it in "P" and back in gear, nothing will change. Going through all ranges has no effect. However, and put it in park, then kill the motor, and start it again, the tranny returns to normal operation.<P>The only other way to describe this phenomena is "  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> " <P>I'm thinking dirty valve bodies. No slipping, no whining. When it's in this "stunned" state, the shifts are consistant, and hard. Almost nice... Except that it won't shift until around 3000-3500rpm (varies by throttle). This makes for some buzzy city driving. I don't know ANY way of explaining WHY THE HELL MY THROTTLE GETS STIFF  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||09-23-2001||07:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Not sure if this is a major problem or not. doesn't seem to be...<P>If at any time during a drive, you floor the gas, and back off in a certain way BEFORE it shifts into second (I haven't quite determined the factors) the throttle will become "heavy" and the shifting characteristics of the tranny change dramatically. The throttle is harder to push (   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  ) and the shifts are delayed, and very hard. It will stay like this for the remainder of the drive. If you stop, and put it in "P" and back in gear, nothing will change. Going through all ranges has no effect. However, and put it in park, then kill the motor, and start it again, the tranny returns to normal operation.<P>The only other way to describe this phenomena is "   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  " <P>I'm thinking dirty valve bodies. No slipping, no whining. When it's in this "stunned" state, the shifts are consistant, and hard. Almost nice... Except that it won't shift until around 3000-3500rpm (varies by throttle). This makes for some buzzy city driving. I don't know ANY way of explaining WHY THE HELL MY THROTTLE GETS STIFF   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Mach!<BR>Check your transmission control cable attachment for frayed cable...<BR><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">    <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||09-23-2001||08:26 PM||||Kick-down cable <I>seems</I> to be OK... It's REALLY weird. More annoying than anything else, plus it chows down on my gas mileage, as I'm running at higher revs...<P>I'm wondering if it's worth slapping a new filter on the tranny... It's got 70k on it, and has never been done. Might be some deposit buildup in the oil journals? I'm confused, but not desparate  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||09-23-2001||11:33 PM||||*bump*||||reg||1
Z||000004||Neal||09-23-2001||11:51 PM||||Im not the expert here, I dont know squat about autos, but id say put in a new fluid, filter and one of them shift kits and see if that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||09-24-2001||01:22 AM||||Mach10 - This is just a guess but it sounds like the pressure combinations of various circuits in the valve body caused by your 'funky operational sequence' is the culprit.  When you shut the car off the pump stops making pressure and allows whatever valve is stuck to return to normal mode.  New fluid may help, but it just may be a combination of 'goop' in the valve body or old weak springs in there.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||09-24-2001||04:25 AM||||87GTZ34: Yep, that's what I thought. I don't think the springs are weak, the trans has VERY few clicks on it for the age. I'm leaning more towards the "goop" theory. Thing is, though, there's no way in HELL I am having the thing flushed. I've noticed that trannies tend to go tits-up soon following a flush. Maybe some fresher fluid will help...||||reg||7
Z||000007||theogre||09-24-2001||08:35 PM||||There's a few ways to do a fluid change. the more fluid replaced the better. Use only new Dexron III fluid. It's better than the older versions.<P>There's one way to do it in my cave. You'll need some spare flesible line, almost anykind will do, and a large pail.<P>You can also drop the pan and once it's off start if for a minute. DO NOT rev it. DO NOT work the shifter. This will likely make a mess since you can't control the fluid like the way I do in the cave article.<P>This may help but don't be surprise if it doesn't there is a known bug in the throttle body of some versions of TH125. It causes the kind of problem you are discribing. GM does have a TSB out on it but I'm not publishing it. If someone messes up the repair then the valve body in the tranny can be easily destroyed. (The TSB requires permanent alterations of the valve body.<BR> The TSB is available thru ALLDATA.)<P>Would a shift kit help? Can't say. There are several available and each is diferent.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||09-24-2001||08:56 PM||||Once again, the venerable Ogre comes to my rescue  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Well, The shift-kit is going in regardless. I do plan on the filter/fluid change. If I wreck the tranny, it just gives me an excuse to slap a 4-spd into it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I think the shift-kit might actually fix it. It adjusts the way fluid pressures are handled inside the tranny. Maybe it will change the characteristics of this bug, too. Who knows, after the SK, maybe it'll only do it if I drop the throttle suddenly at 8000rpm  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>I'll certainly look at your articles. Maybe I'll just do the filter on my own, as opposed to shelling out $40 for it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>The shift kit will go in later, when I have more money.||||reg||7
